### Check first
Open the [caller’s metrics](/#/components/{{componentUrnForUrl}}/metrics) and inspect the last sample of each GenAI client duration count. The chart shows the largest sample age on this caller. If a sample goes stale between 15-second query steps, its displayed age can be up to 15 seconds high. A gap over **{{threshold}}** seconds triggers the check. An unchanged count, including zero, is fresh when its sample timestamp advances.

### Likely causes
The periodic metric reader or Collector stopped exporting, a filter dropped duration, or the worker or target labels changed. Token counts can continue when duration export fails. At a one-minute export interval, the five-minute allowance covers five export periods. It assumes cumulative counts continue to arrive while the caller is idle.

### Fix
Check the metric reader’s effective export interval, temporality and delivery delay. The Pipelines SDK reader can use `OTEL_METRIC_EXPORT_INTERVAL`. Upstream Open WebUI 0.6.41 explicitly sets its host meter reader interval to 10 seconds for both OTLP/HTTP and OTLP/gRPC, so that setting has no effect on the host reader’s interval. If a producer exports only on events or exports slowly, normal idle periods can trigger this check. Restore duration export for the affected caller, instance, instrumentation scope, provider, model and endpoint.

### Verify
A new duration sample renews the target immediately on evaluation. If duration stays absent for fifteen minutes, that target drops out of the check. Other observations can keep the service visible. An empty chart supplies no health result.
